WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.963
Welcome back to Building
Global Secure Access.

2
00:00:02.963 --> 00:00:07.387
This is Chapter 2: the configuration
export, and logs first.

3
00:00:07.387 --> 00:00:10.031
Every later chapter reads the same export,

4
00:00:10.031 --> 00:00:14.638
and nothing is enforced before the logs
exist, so this chapter builds both.

5
00:00:14.638 --> 00:00:19.297
We'll start from Learn's advice to back
the configuration up as Graph JSON,

6
00:00:19.297 --> 00:00:22.304
see what it leaves to you,
read the kit's collector,

7
00:00:22.304 --> 00:00:25.762
its manifest and five baseline
checks, and walk two labs.

8
00:00:25.762 --> 00:00:28.534
The chapter has been
reviewed and approved,

9
00:00:28.534 --> 00:00:32.360
and the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

10
00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:37.422
Why this chapter, now?

11
00:00:37.422 --> 00:00:40.522
The guide's run order is
decision, architecture,

12
00:00:40.522 --> 00:00:42.985
then logging, and only then the client.

13
00:00:42.985 --> 00:00:46.076
So the logs exist before
anything is enforced,

14
00:00:46.076 --> 00:00:49.410
and every later Validate
step has a record to read.

15
00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:54.372
The goal: export the tenant's configuration
in one read-only run whose files an

16
00:00:54.372 --> 00:00:59.800
assessor can re-hash; keep the architecture
record that names every connector group

17
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.436
and DNS suffix; and prove the network logs

18
00:01:02.436 --> 00:01:05.460
reach the workspace before
anyone is in a pilot.

19
00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:09.930
It's for the developers, the platform
owner who keeps the architecture record,

20
00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:13.080
and the security monitor
who owns the workspace.

21
00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:21.791
Learn's operations guidance is to back
the configuration up as Graph JSON,

22
00:01:21.791 --> 00:01:26.043
weekly and before every change, and
the guide's export is that loop.

23
00:01:26.043 --> 00:01:27.804
Each read has a Learn page.

24
00:01:27.804 --> 00:01:31.642
Profiles, signaling and tagging
need Network Access Read All,

25
00:01:31.642 --> 00:01:36.005
and Global Reader or the Global Secure
Access Log Reader can read them.

26
00:01:36.005 --> 00:01:40.705
Named locations and the tenant
restrictions default need Policy Read All.

27
00:01:40.705 --> 00:01:45.655
Connector groups and their members are
the surprise: Learn names only Directory

28
00:01:45.655 --> 00:01:49.793
Read Write All, delegated, so there's
no read-only permission for them.

29
00:01:49.793 --> 00:01:53.405
A connector reports active or
inactive, and its version.

30
00:01:53.405 --> 00:01:58.430
And traffic logs stay thirty days, longer
only through a diagnostic setting.

31
00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:03.405
What does it leave to you?

32
00:02:03.405 --> 00:02:06.079
The guide's export covers nine addresses,

33
00:02:06.079 --> 00:02:10.562
but later chapters also need connector
members, Private Access apps,

34
00:02:10.562 --> 00:02:14.730
licenses and the logs, so the kit
has one collector for every read.

35
00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:17.531
The guide's hash list is
something nobody checks,

36
00:02:17.531 --> 00:02:21.943
so the kit's manifest is checked every
time a later step reads the collection.

37
00:02:21.943 --> 00:02:26.213
The connector reads need a write
permission, so the call list says so,

38
00:02:26.213 --> 00:02:29.492
the reader only sends GET,
and the lab signs out after.

39
00:02:29.492 --> 00:02:32.969
A diagnostic setting created
by hand can lose a category,

40
00:02:32.969 --> 00:02:36.593
and an architecture record in
prose drifts from the tenant.

41
00:02:36.593 --> 00:02:38.310
The kit checks both.

42
00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.060
Here's the code.

43
00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:46.763
The collector reads every declared
call for the areas you name,

44
00:02:46.763 --> 00:02:51.391
all of them by default: seventeen calls,
twenty-four reads in the fixture tenant,

45
00:02:51.391 --> 00:02:54.025
because connector
members are read once per

46
00:02:54.025 --> 00:02:56.660
group and the guide's
seven queries one by one.

47
00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:59.436
It writes each read, one collection file,

48
00:02:59.436 --> 00:03:03.810
and a manifest with each call's
permission and every file's hash.

49
00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:07.027
Two reads aren't verified
against a Learn page yet,

50
00:03:07.027 --> 00:03:09.773
and live runs refuse
them unless you say so.

51
00:03:09.773 --> 00:03:15.180
Then five baseline checks: each connector
group in the architecture record with two

52
00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:20.125
hosts, the forest in the DNS suffixes,
the workspace named, the export complete,

53
00:03:20.125 --> 00:03:24.650
and the diagnostic setting sending
all four network categories.

54
00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:29.325
Who does what?

55
00:03:29.325 --> 00:03:31.899
The platform owner keeps the architecture

56
00:03:31.899 --> 00:03:34.687
record and runs the export
before every change.

57
00:03:34.687 --> 00:03:38.638
The security monitor owns the
workspace and the diagnostic setting.

58
00:03:38.638 --> 00:03:41.735
The security officer
keeps the export folders,

59
00:03:41.735 --> 00:03:44.674
in the evidence store,
not on a personal drive.

60
00:03:44.674 --> 00:03:49.571
The reader holds Network Access Read All
and Policy Read All for the configuration,

61
00:03:49.571 --> 00:03:52.409
and Directory Read Write
All for the connectors,

62
00:03:52.409 --> 00:03:54.750
so the lab signs out right after the read.

63
00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:59.005
Creating the diagnostic setting takes
Security Administrator, and roles are

64
00:03:59.005 --> 00:04:03.690
activated through privileged identity
management for the change window only.

65
00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.752
Lab 1, again in the kit's
folder with no tenant.

66
00:04:10.752 --> 00:04:13.290
Step 1 lists what the baseline reads.

67
00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:17.033
Step 2 collects the whole
pilot tenant: seventeen calls,

68
00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:22.077
each with its least-privileged permission
and its beta and verified flags.

69
00:04:22.077 --> 00:04:25.180
The Private Access apps
and the tenant restrictions

70
00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:28.565
partners are the two not yet
verified against a Learn page.

71
00:04:28.565 --> 00:04:31.865
Twenty-four reads in all,
eighteen files hashed.

72
00:04:31.865 --> 00:04:37.400
Step 3 checks the folder against its
manifest: all eighteen files match.

73
00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:43.538
Step 4 runs the baseline checks.

74
00:04:43.538 --> 00:04:48.390
Both connector groups are in the
architecture record with two hosts each,

75
00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:52.288
the forest is in the DNS suffixes,
and the workspace is named.

76
00:04:52.288 --> 00:04:57.075
One failure: the diagnostic setting doesn't
send the remote network health logs.

77
00:04:57.075 --> 00:05:01.305
Step 5 reads the setting's categories
as the collector saved them:

78
00:05:01.305 --> 00:05:03.812
three on, and remote network health off.

79
00:05:03.812 --> 00:05:07.313
No branch is on a remote
network yet, but when one is,

80
00:05:07.313 --> 00:05:10.750
its health would never
reach the workspace.

81
00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:16.825
Step 6 is the one to remember.

82
00:05:16.825 --> 00:05:21.200
Copy the collection, add one space
to one file, and try to read it.

83
00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:25.812
The read is refused: the licenses
file changed since its manifest.

84
00:05:25.812 --> 00:05:29.852
Every later chapter reads the
collection through that same check,

85
00:05:29.852 --> 00:05:32.863
so an edited export can't
quietly become evidence.

86
00:05:32.863 --> 00:05:37.388
Step 7 runs this chapter's tests:
eight passed, none failed.

87
00:05:37.388 --> 00:05:39.590
Then delete the output folder.

88
00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:46.957
Lab 2 uses a test tenant and
an Azure test subscription

89
00:05:46.957 --> 00:05:50.252
with a Log Analytics
workspace, never production.

90
00:05:50.252 --> 00:05:54.361
Turn the network logs on first,
with the guide's own command:

91
00:05:54.361 --> 00:05:57.528
one diagnostic setting,
all four categories.

92
00:05:57.528 --> 00:06:02.116
Then sign in to Graph as a person with
the baseline's permissions and collect.

93
00:06:02.116 --> 00:06:04.631
The two unverified reads
need an extra switch,

94
00:06:04.631 --> 00:06:08.340
and you compare their results with
the portal before trusting them.

95
00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:11.378
The logging check passes
once the setting exists.

96
00:06:11.378 --> 00:06:14.950
Back it out by signing out first,
because the session can write,

97
00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:18.664
and delete the setting only if this
test tenant shouldn't keep it.

98
00:06:18.664 --> 00:06:21.030
The authors haven't run this lab.

99
00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:27.818
The export with its
permissions and hashes supports

100
00:06:27.818 --> 00:06:30.718
baseline configuration
and component inventory.

101
00:06:30.718 --> 00:06:35.167
The architecture record supports boundary
protection and information flow.

102
00:06:35.167 --> 00:06:39.963
Logs in the workspace before the pilot
support event logging and audit record

103
00:06:39.963 --> 00:06:44.980
generation, and a refused, changed export
supports protecting audit information.

104
00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:49.081
The key security indicators are
FedRAMP's, and which output supports

105
00:06:49.081 --> 00:06:52.967
which is the program's mapping;
the assessor decides the controls.

106
00:06:52.967 --> 00:06:55.435
The dangers: a pilot before the logs,

107
00:06:55.435 --> 00:07:00.455
a write-capable session left open,
and an edited export used as evidence.

108
00:07:00.455 --> 00:07:03.541
You're done when the setting
existed before the pilot,

109
00:07:03.541 --> 00:07:05.855
and the export runs before every change.

110
00:07:05.855 --> 00:07:08.580
Next, Chapter 3: the client.
